  "live_records": {
    "q-278480": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278480",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14285227/assets/c5672a0655ffd9ab3e098250e70f5bb782d14fd53c327ff7d14a1c0d798d25c4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-3,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=4,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
