    "q-278477": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278477",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14285227/assets/fb178149bac4d1fcfd0690b7005f263c3647488ac86ed70f1143540e23e04b6e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(2,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y+2)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
